{
  "kind": "protocol-inflation-table-spec",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "spec_id": "protocol-inflation-v6",
  "primary_metric": "sharpe",
  "notes": [
    "Each row isolates one evaluation shortcut by holding the panel, strategy,",
    "allocator, cost model, and execution rule fixed and varying only the",
    "reporting choice. The 'reported' column is what a paper using the shortcut",
    "would print; the 'honest' column is the same underlying runs read under the",
    "stricter rule. Inflation is reported - honest.",
    "No row selects a strategy. Every run named here was already executed and",
    "documented before this spec existed; the spec only fixes how they are read.",
    "v2 adds rows measured on the Track-B confirmation panel, which was sealed when the v1 rows were computed. Those rows are an out-of-sample replication of the inflation measurement itself, not new candidate evidence.",
    "v3 adds the Track-B secondary-window rows. That window is the sharpest case in the table: reporting one seed yields an apparently marginal positive result, and every honest reading of the same runs removes it.",
    "v4 adds the period-mismatch row surfaced by the quarterly robustness cell: a model with a long warm-up starts later than its baselines, so reading both off one table compares different market periods while every individual run remains correct.",
    "v5 adds the training-budget row. The neural comparator was frozen at five epochs while its config declared twenty; running the declared budget raises the development median above equal weight and lowers the held-out median below it, so the shortcut measured here is reading a development window at a budget that does not transfer.",
    "v6 adds the compound cell: one run read under the development window, the best seed and zero costs at once, against the honest reading of the same model."
  ],
